=== Post-quantum key exchange ===
To protect against [[Harvest now, decrypt later|"harvest now, decrypt later"]] attacks by future [[quantum computer]]s, hybrid [[post-quantum cryptography|post-quantum]] key agreement schemes have been deployed for TLS 1.3. These combine a classical [[Elliptic-curve Diffie–Hellman|ECDHE]] exchange with [[ML-KEM]], the lattice-based [[key encapsulation mechanism]] standardized by [[NIST]] as FIPS 203 in August 2024.<ref>{{cite web |title=FIPS 203: Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard |publisher=National Institute of Standards and Technology |date=13 August 2024 |url=https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final}}</ref> The [[IETF]] TLS working group defines the hybrid groups X25519MLKEM768, SecP256r1MLKEM768 and SecP384r1MLKEM1024 in an Internet-Draft,<ref>{{cite web |title=Post-quantum hybrid ECDHE-MLKEM Key Agreement for TLSv1.3 |publisher=IETF |url=https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-ecdhe-mlkem/}}</ref> alongside a general framework for hybrid key exchange in TLS 1.3<ref>{{cite web |title=Hybrid key exchange in TLS 1.3 |publisher=IETF |url=https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-hybrid-design/}}</ref> and a specification for standalone (non-hybrid) ML-KEM key agreement.<ref>{{cite web |title=ML-KEM Post-Quantum Key Agreement for TLS 1.3 |publisher=IETF |url=https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-mlkem/}}</ref>

X25519MLKEM768 (and its predecessor X25519Kyber768) has been enabled by default in [[Google Chrome]] since version 131 (November 2024)<ref>{{cite web |title=Advancing Our Amazing Bet on Asymmetric Cryptography |publisher=Chromium Blog |date=May 2024 |url=https://blog.chromium.org/2024/05/advancing-our-amazing-bet-on-asymmetric.html}}</ref> and [[Firefox]] since version 132,<ref>{{cite web |title=Firefox 132.0 Release Notes |publisher=Mozilla |date=October 2024 |url=https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/132.0/releasenotes/}}</ref> and is supported by server-side implementations including [[OpenSSL]] 3.5,<ref>{{cite web |title=OpenSSL 3.5 Release Notes |publisher=OpenSSL |url=https://openssl-library.org/news/openssl-3.5-notes/}}</ref> [[BoringSSL]] and [[Cloudflare]].<ref>{{cite web |title=The state of the post-quantum Internet |publisher=Cloudflare |date=March 2024 |url=https://blog.cloudflare.com/pq-2024/}}</ref>

