==[[IETF]] [[Request for comments]]==

===Primary standards===
The current approved version of (D)TLS is version 1.3, which is specified in:
*{{Sum RFC|9846|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|9147|ref=yes}}

The current standards replaces these former versions:
*{{Sum RFC|5246|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6347|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4346|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|2246|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6101|ref=yes}}
*[//tools.ietf.org/html/draft-hickman-netscape-ssl-00 Internet Draft (1995)]: "The SSL Protocol"

===Extensions===
Other [[Request for Comments|RFCs]] subsequently extended (D)TLS.

Extensions to (D)TLS 1.3 include:
*{{Sum RFC|9367|ref=yes}}

Extensions to (D)TLS 1.2 include:
*{{Sum RFC|5288|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5289|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5746|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5878|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5932|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6066|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Includes [[Server Name Indication]] and [[OCSP stapling]].}}
*{{Sum RFC|6091|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6176|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6209|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6347|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=The definition is now part of the DTLS 1.3 specification.}}
*{{Sum RFC|6367|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|6460|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Support for Suite B Cryptography has been discontinued by the [[National Security Agency]].}}
*{{Sum RFC|6655|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7027|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7251|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7301|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7366|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7465|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7507|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7568|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7627|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|7685|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|8422|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|9189|ref=yes}}

Extensions to (D)TLS 1.1 include:
*{{Sum RFC|4366|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Describes both a set of specific extensions and a generic extension mechanism.}}
*{{Sum RFC|4492|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4680|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4681|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4785|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5054|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Defines the [[TLS-SRP]] ciphersuites.}}
*{{Sum RFC|5077|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5081|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|5216|ref=yes}}

Extensions to TLS 1.0 include:
*{{Sum RFC|2595|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Specifies an extension to the IMAP, POP3 and ACAP services that allow the server and client to use transport-layer security to provide private, authenticated communication over the Internet.}}
*{{Sum RFC|2712|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=The 40-bit cipher suites defined in this memo appear only for the purpose of documenting the fact that those cipher suite codes have already been assigned.}}
*{{Sum RFC|2817|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Explains how to use the [[HTTP/1.1 Upgrade header|Upgrade mechanism in HTTP/1.1]] to initiate Transport Layer Security (TLS) over an existing TCP connection. This allows unsecured and secured HTTP traffic to share the same ''well known'' port (in this case, http: at 80 rather than https: at 443).}}
*{{Sum RFC|2818|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Distinguishes secured traffic from insecure traffic by the use of a different 'server port'.}}
*{{Sum RFC|3207|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Specifies an extension to the SMTP service that allows an SMTP server and client to use transport-layer security to provide private, authenticated communication over the Internet.}}
*{{Sum RFC|3268|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Adds [[Advanced Encryption Standard]] (AES) cipher suites to the previously existing symmetric ciphers.}}
*{{Sum RFC|3546|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Adds a mechanism for negotiating protocol extensions during session initialisation and defines some extensions.}}
*{{Sum RFC|3749|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Specifies the framework for compression methods and the [[DEFLATE]] compression method.}}
*{{Sum RFC|3943|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4132|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4162|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4217|ref=yes}}
*{{Sum RFC|4279|ref=yes}}{{Block indent|text=Adds three sets of new cipher suites for the TLS protocol to support authentication based on pre-shared keys.}}

